    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 168\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 5752, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to require school district compliance with physical educa-\n      tion instruction  requirements  in  elementary  grades  in  public\n      schools to be reported to the department of education\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  establish  a  reporting  protocol for public school\ndistricts  to  report  their  compliance  with  the  physical  education\nrequirements for elementary grades. School districts that fail to comply\nwith requirements would prepare a corrective action plan for approval by\nthe  school  district's  board  of education and those failing to report\nwould be listed on the State Education Department's website.\n \n  I continue to support the intent of this bill. Physical education is a\ncritical part of a student's overall education. Without mandatory  phys-\nical  education  classes, many students would not receive regular, phys-\nical activity. Physical education classes promote physical  fitness  and\nmotor skills development, and have been shown to improve overall academ-\nic performance.\n \n  However,  this  bill  is  identical to the bill I vetoed last year and\nthus suffers from the same fiscal and policy concerns (Veto No.  268  of\n2016).  As  in  the  previous bill, significant State resources would be\nrequired to compile, review, analyze, and report on data collected  from\nevery  elementary  school  in  the  State. This would pose a significant\nburden on school districts without any funds to support it, which  would\nultimately  be borne by local taxpayers. This is unnecessary, especially\nwhen the information the Legislature seeks is already  provided  to  the\nState.  School  districts  are  already  required to file plans with the\nState Education Department detailing their physical  education  program-\nming.  They  must  also file building condition survey reports detailing\nthe instructional facilities of the  school  district  and  adequacy  of\nspace, which includes physical education space.\n \n  For these reasons, I am again constrained to veto this bill. Given the\nimportance of physical education to our students, however, I remain open\nto  discussing this proposal in the context of the annual budget negoti-\nations, where such funding decisions can be made.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
